**pr-body.txt**:
```
BLUF: this PR changes one file, web/package-lock.json, and moves two transitive development dependencies: undici 8.11.2 -> 8.12.3 and zod 4.6.5 -> 4.6.7. web/package.json is unchanged. It carries the same two bumps as #120 (undici) and #121 (zod) so that the code scan runs on a commit pushed by this project's own account. It supersedes #120 and #121; they are to be closed unmerged once this PR lands.

What changes
- node_modules/undici 8.11.2 -> 8.12.3 (version, resolved, integrity). Its only parent is jsdom 30.1.2.
- node_modules/zod 4.6.5 -> 4.6.7 (version, resolved, integrity). Its parents are eslint-plugin-react-hooks 7.1.1 and, as a peer, zod-validation-error 4.0.2.
- Lockfile blob after: 7c7d4608f669a19e4b52c963cdb4debb81d6f879. Paths changed: 1. Entries changed: 2. All resolved hosts are registry.npmjs.org.

Why a new PR
#120 and #121 were opened by the dependency bot and their heads have no code-scanning analysis (read on 4 Nov 2026). A commit reaches main only after code scanning has run on it in a PR, so the same two changes are pushed here under the project's own identity. Nothing is bypassed.

What was measured
- On an identical lockfile (same blob, checked outside this branch), npm ci, lint, npm test and the production build all exit 0; 143 test files, 1398 tests, all passed, names equal to main's. Those commands were NOT re-run on this branch for this PR; the CI run on this PR is the check for that.
- No source file imports undici or zod (0 files each); neither appears in the production bundle (0 hits in web/dist).

Security text relayed from #120 and #121 (RELAYED from the upstream release notes quoted in those PRs; not verified here)
- undici 8.12.2 release notes list security fixes. #120 prints five of them: GHSA-qrxm-fqhp-jwvm (CVE-2026-87018), GHSA-2m5v-pch6-74hx (CVE-2026-109383), GHSA-4w7w-5xrc-9h26 (CVE-2026-109829), GHSA-xjj6-f33q-929c (CVE-2026-109869), GHSA-55wc-3pg8-jfw6 (CVE-2026-87029). They concern client-side network behaviour (TLS, redirects with a cache, content coding, SRI in fetch, the decompress interceptor). The upstream notes list eight in all.
- zod 4.6.6 release notes (quoted in #121) say six malicious commits were pushed to the upstream main on 21 September with a compromised git credential, were reverted the same day, have been removed from the history of main, and were never published to npm; the npm provenance for 4.6.6 is named in that note.
- undici is a dependency of jsdom, which the test workers load; neither package is shipped code (see above).

Landing: squash with the head pinned. Close #120 and #121 unmerged afterwards, with a pointer to this PR.
```

